{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-275159",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14237395/assets/b6ec4332891a4c7684be8de2ca94bf24a1d9abd5170d9fe38ad3a1346afae56a.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{7}{9})=0.661\\,043…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=2\\pi -0.661\\,043≈5.62,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
